{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014PFWSt37kcwfrfYbqwP1vx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted experimental results for the \"worst\" tier scenario for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment_plan. Let me identify which experiments are still in \"planned\" status:\n\n1. plan_001 - feature_ablation_and_trend (status: planned)\n2. plan_002 - cross_generator_evaluation (status: planned)\n3. plan_006 - paraphrastic_comparison (status: planned)\n4. plan_009 - finer_grained_ordinal (status: planned)\n\n\n5. plan_012 - end_to_end_classifier (status: planned)\n\nActually, I need to double-check this against the registry since many of these experiments appear to have already been completed. Looking more carefully, plan_001, plan_002, plan_006, and plan_009 all show as done in the registry despite being marked as planned in the experiment_plan, so I should verify which ones are actually still pending before generating results.\n\nLet me review the experiment_plan status fields directly to see which are genuinely marked as planned versus done.\n\nLooking at the registry, I notice there's a discrepancy—some experiments marked as \"planned\" in the status field actually have completed results recorded. The user is asking me to generate predicted results for the planned experiments, which appears to be a hypothetical exercise based on the worst-tier scenario defined in Phase 1, even though some of these experiments may have already run in reality. I need to focus on what the experiment_plan document itself designates as planned: plan_001, plan_002, plan_006, plan_009, and plan_012.\n\nNow I'm working through what the worst-tier performance would look like for each of these five experiments, keeping in mind the worst-tier definition involves held-out F1 scores below 0.60 for new families and inconsistent performance across certain architectures like Gemma. I'm shifting the framing from a universal depth signal to something more nuanced—architecture-conditional responses. Now I need to think through how each planned experiment would play out under this worst-case scenario. Plan_001 focuses on ablation within the MVP data using only Pythia models, so it might still show reasonable results since it's staying within the same family, but I should expect the differential responses to be messier and less clean than the initial findings suggested. The real degradation shows up when moving to new generators, so this first experiment could mask some of the underlying fragility.\n\nPlan_002 is where things fall apart—expanding across five generators from three different families reveals that OLMo and Qwen models show much weaker signals, and the LOGO F1 scores drop significantly for non-Pythia architectures.\n\nFor the paraphrastic comparison in plan_006, both paraphrastic and continuation chains show surprisingly weak signals under the worst-case scenario, which aligns with the attractor theory prediction. The finer-grained ordinal classification in plan_009 struggles even at three classes for some generators. The end-to-end classifier in plan_012 might capture some learned features that handcrafted ones miss, but it's still constrained by the underlying weak signal across certain architectures.\n\nLooking at the new generators being added—Llama-3.2-3B, Gemma-2-2B, and Mistral-7B—these would bring the total to nine generators in the worst-tier scenario.\n\nNow I need to focus on generating worst-tier predictions for each of the planned experiments: plan_001, plan_002, plan_006, plan_009, and plan_012.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28762,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":28762},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_availab